        "result": "The check now returns **violates**. Graph is at revision 7. The rule was not touched.\n\n**Check result** (evaluate `port-rule` against `port-instances`, three reports)\n\n| Instance | Owners reported | Distinct owners | Outcome |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| (host-a, 8080) | service-a, service-c | 2 | violates |\n| (host-b, 8080) | service-b | 1 | satisfies |\n\n**The actual witness**\n\nThe violating subject is the ordered pair (host-a, 8080). Under your fixture identity, the original observation and the new one name the same port, and the cardinality slot counts two distinct owned-by targets where the rule demands exactly one. Host B is unaffected because it is a different port under (host, number).\n\nOne fidelity note: the checker's top-level witnesses array came back empty. The witness is carried in the per-subject entry with distinct_count 2 and outcome violates. I recorded that as it was returned rather than inventing a witness list.\n\n**What I wrote**\n\n- `port-observation-service-c-source`: verbatim synthetic source of your sentence, attributed to you.\n- `port-observations`: third report appended, first two unchanged, still declared complete. An extracted-from edge binds it to the new source.\n- `port-instances`: rebuilt from the three reports under the unchanged identity key.\n- `port-rule-check-host-number-r6`: stored receipt of the violates result, with a tests edge to the rule and a revises edge to the earlier receipt.\n- `port-rule-check-host-number`: kept, marked historical with the reason.\n- `port-rule`: unchanged. Same text, same pattern, still accepted with pattern standing proposed.\n\n**Interpretation limits**\n\nThis is a bounded evaluation on synthetic fixture data. It shows that these three reports, read under (host, number) identity, cannot all be true alongside the exactly-one rule. It does not say which of the rule, the identity, or the new observation is wrong. Whether \"owner\" means the same thing for service-a and service-c on host A is still the unconfirmed question from the last turn, and it is now the most likely place a real-world resolution would come from.",
